window.QUIZ_BANK['junit'] = (window.QUIZ_BANK['junit']||[]).concat([
{id:4921,topic:'junit',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does @ExtendWith do in JUnit 5?',options:['Extends the test class with inheritance','Registers one or more extensions declaratively on a test class or method','Enables parallel test execution','Adds extra assertion methods'],answer:1,explanation:'@ExtendWith(SomeExtension.class) registers a JUnit 5 extension on a test class or method. Extensions can hook into the test lifecycle, resolve parameters, handle exceptions, and more. Multiple extensions can be registered by passing an array.'},
{id:4922,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What interface must a class implement to be a JUnit 5 extension?',options:['TestExtension','Extension (a marker interface from org.junit.jupiter.api.extension)','TestRule','TestWatcher'],answer:1,explanation:'org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.Extension is the root marker interface for all JUnit 5 extensions. Extensions implement one or more specific callback interfaces (BeforeEachCallback, ParameterResolver, etc.) that extend this marker interface.'},
{id:4923,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the BeforeAllCallback extension interface allow you to do?',options:['Replaces @BeforeAll in tests','Adds before-all logic from an external extension class that runs before any test in the class','Prevents @BeforeAll from running','Runs after each test'],answer:1,explanation:'BeforeAllCallback defines a single method beforeAll(ExtensionContext context) that JUnit invokes before any test method in a class. Extensions implementing this interface can perform expensive one-time setup, like starting a server, without modifying the test class itself.'},
{id:4924,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the AfterAllCallback extension interface allow?',options:['Replaces @AfterAll annotations','Runs after the last test in a class from an extension, enabling one-time teardown in extension code','Cancels pending tests','Runs after each test method'],answer:1,explanation:'AfterAllCallback defines afterAll(ExtensionContext context) which JUnit calls after all tests in a class have finished. It is used for tearing down shared resources set up in BeforeAllCallback, such as stopping embedded servers or closing connection pools.'},
{id:4925,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the BeforeEachCallback extension interface provide?',options:['Replaces @BeforeEach in the test class','Executes setup code from the extension before each test method, complementing the @BeforeEach in the test class','Skips the @BeforeEach annotation','Resets test state after each test'],answer:1,explanation:'BeforeEachCallback.beforeEach(ExtensionContext context) is invoked by JUnit before every test method (after the class-level @BeforeAll but before @BeforeEach). Extensions use it for per-test setup like resetting mocks, injecting fields, or starting transactions.'},
{id:4926,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the AfterEachCallback extension interface provide?',options:['Runs code after each test method from the extension, complementing @AfterEach','Replaces @AfterEach in test classes','Only runs after failing tests','Runs before each assertion'],answer:0,explanation:'AfterEachCallback.afterEach(ExtensionContext context) is called by JUnit after every test method. Extensions implement this to perform per-test cleanup such as rolling back transactions, verifying mock interactions, or releasing resources that were set up in beforeEach.'},
{id:4927,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the TestExecutionExceptionHandler extension interface allow?',options:['Prevents all exceptions from failing tests','Intercepts exceptions thrown by test methods, allowing the extension to handle, suppress, or re-throw them','Converts checked exceptions to unchecked','Logs exceptions to a file'],answer:1,explanation:'TestExecutionExceptionHandler.handleTestExecutionException(ExtensionContext, Throwable) is called when a test throws an exception. The extension can examine the exception, potentially swallow it (causing the test to pass), re-throw it, or throw a different exception.'},
{id:4928,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does the ParameterResolver extension interface enable in JUnit 5?',options:['Converts method parameters to other types','Allows extensions to inject custom parameters into test constructors, @BeforeEach, @AfterEach, @BeforeAll, @AfterAll, and @Test methods','Provides default values for null parameters','Validates parameter types at compile time'],answer:1,explanation:'ParameterResolver defines supportsParameter(ParameterContext, ExtensionContext) and resolveParameter(ParameterContext, ExtensionContext). JUnit calls these to inject custom objects into any method that JUnit manages, enabling dependency injection without a DI framework in tests.'},
{id:4929,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does the InvocationInterceptor extension interface allow?',options:['Logs each test invocation','Intercepts calls to test methods, constructors, and lifecycle methods, enabling AOP-style wrapping like running code on a different thread or inside a transaction','Prevents test methods from being called','Records invocation metrics'],answer:1,explanation:'InvocationInterceptor provides hooks for intercepting invocations of test methods, lifecycle methods, and test class constructors. Each hook receives an Invocation object whose proceed() method executes the actual invocation, enabling wrapping patterns like Swing EDT dispatch or transaction management.'},
{id:4930,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is @RegisterExtension used for in JUnit 5?',options:['Registers an extension globally','Registers an extension programmatically as a field in the test class, allowing constructor injection or field configuration','Replaces @ExtendWith','Marks a class as an extension'],answer:1,explanation:'@RegisterExtension on a field in a test class registers the extension instance programmatically. This allows extensions to be configured with constructor arguments or builder patterns. A static field creates a class-scoped extension; an instance field creates a method-scoped extension.'},
{id:4931,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between @ExtendWith and @RegisterExtension in JUnit 5?',options:['They are identical','@ExtendWith is declarative using a class reference; @RegisterExtension is programmatic using a field instance, allowing constructor configuration','@RegisterExtension is for global extensions only','@ExtendWith supports more lifecycle hooks'],answer:1,explanation:'@ExtendWith references an extension class and JUnit instantiates it; you cannot configure constructor arguments. @RegisterExtension uses a field holding a pre-configured extension instance, giving full control over initialization while still participating in the JUnit lifecycle.'},
{id:4932,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does auto-registration of JUnit 5 extensions via ServiceLoader work?',options:['Place the extension class in a META-INF/junit-extensions folder','Create a file META-INF/services/org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.Extension listing the FQCN of the extension; JUnit loads it via ServiceLoader','Annotate the extension with @AutoRegister','Add the extension to junit-platform.properties'],answer:1,explanation:'JUnit 5 supports automatic extension registration through Java\'s ServiceLoader mechanism. Create META-INF/services/org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.Extension containing the fully qualified class name(s) of your extension(s). Enable discovery by setting junit.jupiter.extensions.autodetection.enabled=true.'},
{id:4933,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What configuration property must be set to true for ServiceLoader-based auto-registration of extensions to work?',options:['junit.jupiter.extensions.register.auto=true','junit.jupiter.extensions.autodetection.enabled=true','junit.platform.autodetect=true','junit.extensions.spi=true'],answer:1,explanation:'Auto-detection is disabled by default. Set junit.jupiter.extensions.autodetection.enabled=true in junit-platform.properties (or as a JVM system property). Only then will JUnit discover extensions registered via META-INF/services/org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.Extension.'},
{id:4934,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledOnOs do in JUnit 5?',options:['Enables a test only when a specific OS patch is installed','Runs the test only on the specified operating system(s); the test is disabled on all other OS','Sets the OS for the embedded test environment','Enables cross-platform test execution'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledOnOs(OS.LINUX) (or @EnabledOnOs({OS.MAC, OS.WINDOWS})) causes the test to be skipped on any OS not listed. OS values include LINUX, MAC, WINDOWS, AIX, SOLARIS, and OTHER. It is in the org.junit.jupiter.api.condition package.'},
{id:4935,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DisabledOnOs do in JUnit 5?',options:['Disables JUnit on the specified OS','Skips the annotated test when running on the specified operating system(s)','Marks the test as unsupported on the OS','Disables all tests in the class on specific OS'],answer:1,explanation:'@DisabledOnOs(OS.WINDOWS) is the inverse of @EnabledOnOs. The test is skipped when running on the specified operating system(s) and runs normally on all other operating systems. Useful for tests with OS-specific behavior that cannot be made cross-platform.'},
{id:4936,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledOnJre do in JUnit 5?',options:['Runs only on JRE installations, not JDK','Enables the test only on the specified Java Runtime Environment version(s)','Sets the JRE version for the test','Checks if a specific JRE is installed'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledOnJre(JRE.JAVA_11) enables a test only when running on the specified JRE version(s). JRE enum values include JAVA_8, JAVA_9, JAVA_11, JAVA_17, JAVA_21, and JAVA_24 among others. Use @DisabledOnJre for the inverse behavior.'},
{id:4937,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledIfSystemProperty do in JUnit 5?',options:['Enables a test if a JUnit system property is present','Enables the test only when the specified JVM system property matches the given regex pattern','Reads test data from system properties','Sets a system property for the test'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledIfSystemProperty(named="os.arch", matches=".*64.*") enables the test only when the JVM system property "os.arch" matches the given regex. The test is skipped if the property is absent or its value does not match. @DisabledIfSystemProperty is the inverse.'},
{id:4938,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable do in JUnit 5?',options:['Enables a test if any environment variable is set','Enables the test only when the specified environment variable matches the provided regex pattern','Reads test parameters from environment variables','Sets an environment variable for the test duration'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable(named="CI", matches="true") enables the test only when the environment variable "CI" exists and its value matches the regex "true". This is useful for running certain tests only in CI/CD environments.'},
{id:4939,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does @EnabledIf in JUnit 5 (5.7+) allow?',options:['Enables tests based on a SpEL expression','Enables the test based on the return value of a method or static method reference on the test class','Enables tests if a specific class is on the classpath','Enables tests if a specific port is open'],answer:1,explanation:'@EnabledIf("methodName") in JUnit 5.7+ enables the test only when the specified method returns true. The method must return boolean and be accessible from the test class. This provides fine-grained programmatic condition logic beyond the built-in condition annotations.'},
{id:4940,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which JUnit 5 extension is used to integrate Mockito with JUnit 5?',options:['@RunWith(MockitoJUnitRunner.class)','@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)','@MockitoTest','@EnableMockito'],answer:1,explanation:'MockitoExtension (from mockito-junit-jupiter artifact) is registered with @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class). It initializes @Mock, @Spy, @Captor, and @InjectMocks fields, and validates mocks after each test—the JUnit 5 equivalent of MockitoJUnitRunner.'},
{id:4941,topic:'junit',difficulty:'beginner',question:'What does the @Mock annotation do when used with MockitoExtension in JUnit 5?',options:['Creates a real instance of the class','Creates a Mockito mock of the annotated field\'s type, replacing it with a fake object','Injects the mock into a Spring context','Marks a field as thread-safe'],answer:1,explanation:'@Mock creates a Mockito mock for the annotated field. With @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class), Mockito initializes mocks before each test and validates them afterwards. No manual Mockito.mock() call or MockitoAnnotations.openMocks() is needed.'},
{id:4942,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @InjectMocks do in a JUnit 5 test with MockitoExtension?',options:['Injects the test class into a Spring context','Creates an instance of the annotated class and injects all @Mock and @Spy fields into it via constructor, setter, or field injection','Makes all mocks available globally','Replaces the test class constructor'],answer:1,explanation:'@InjectMocks creates an instance of the annotated type and injects @Mock and @Spy fields into it. Mockito tries constructor injection first, then setter injection, then field injection. It eliminates boilerplate new SUT(mockDep) instantiation in tests.'},
{id:4943,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Spy do in Mockito with JUnit 5?',options:['Creates a complete mock that stubs all methods','Creates a partial mock (spy) that wraps a real object, calling real methods unless explicitly stubbed','Injects a spy into Spring context','Creates an immutable test double'],answer:1,explanation:'@Spy creates a Mockito spy—a real object instance whose individual methods can be stubbed while unstubbed methods delegate to real implementations. With MockitoExtension, @Spy fields are initialized automatically before each test.'},
{id:4944,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Captor do in Mockito with JUnit 5?',options:['Captures the return value of a mocked method','Creates an ArgumentCaptor for the specified type, enabling inspection of arguments passed to mocked method calls','Records all method invocations on a mock','Captures exceptions thrown during mock interactions'],answer:1,explanation:'@Captor creates an ArgumentCaptor instance. After a mocked method is called, argumentCaptor.getValue() (or getValues() for multiple invocations) lets you inspect the exact arguments that were passed to the mock, enabling detailed behavior verification.'},
{id:4945,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does @MockitoSettings(strictness = Strictness.STRICT_STUBS) do in JUnit 5?',options:['Enables strict mode for Spring beans','Configures Mockito strictness for the test class; STRICT_STUBS detects unnecessary stubbings and mismatched argument stubbings, failing the test','Disables all Mockito features','Makes all mocks final'],answer:1,explanation:'@MockitoSettings configures Mockito behavior on the test class level. STRICT_STUBS (also the default strictness of MockitoExtension) detects unnecessary stubbings (stubbings not called during the test) and potential argument mismatches, helping keep tests clean and maintainable.'},
{id:4946,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @SpringBootTest do in a JUnit 5 test?',options:['Replaces @ExtendWith for all tests','Loads the full Spring ApplicationContext for integration tests, starting a Spring Boot application context','Mocks all Spring beans','Runs tests on the Spring Boot embedded server only'],answer:1,explanation:'@SpringBootTest triggers Spring Boot\'s test support, loading the complete ApplicationContext. It auto-detects the @SpringBootApplication class. By default no server is started; use webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT or DEFINED_PORT to start an embedded server for web tests.'},
{id:4947,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @WebMvcTest do in a Spring Boot JUnit 5 test?',options:['Tests only the web layer by loading only MVC-related components (controllers, filters, @ControllerAdvice) without the full context','Loads the full application context','Tests the database layer only','Replaces MockMvc with a real HTTP client'],answer:0,explanation:'@WebMvcTest is a slice test annotation that loads only the Spring MVC layer: controllers, @ControllerAdvice, filters, and MVC configuration. Service and repository beans are not loaded. Paired with @MockBean to mock service dependencies.'},
{id:4948,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DataJpaTest do in a Spring Boot JUnit 5 test?',options:['Loads the full Spring context and tests REST endpoints','Configures an in-memory database, scans @Entity classes and JPA repositories, and runs each test in a transaction that is rolled back','Tests Spring Data REST endpoints','Mocks the EntityManager'],answer:1,explanation:'@DataJpaTest is a JPA slice test. It auto-configures an embedded database (like H2), configures Spring Data JPA repositories and the EntityManager, and wraps each test in a transaction rolled back after the test to prevent data pollution.'},
{id:4949,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @MockBean do in a Spring Boot test?',options:['Creates a Mockito mock and injects it into the test class only','Adds a Mockito mock to the Spring ApplicationContext, replacing any existing bean of the same type','Creates a mock that is not managed by Spring','Mocks the bean factory itself'],answer:1,explanation:'@MockBean creates a Mockito mock and registers it as a Spring bean, replacing any existing bean of that type in the context. It is reset between tests. Used in @WebMvcTest and @SpringBootTest to replace real service/repository beans with controllable mocks.'},
{id:4950,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @SpyBean do in a Spring Boot test?',options:['Creates a Mockito spy wrapping the real Spring bean in the context, allowing stubbing while preserving real method calls','Replaces the Spring bean with a pure mock','Creates a spy outside the Spring context','Marks a Spring bean as read-only'],answer:0,explanation:'@SpyBean wraps an existing Spring bean with a Mockito spy. Real methods are called unless stubbed. It is useful when you want to verify interactions with a real implementation while still having the ability to selectively stub certain behaviors.'},
{id:4951,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is MockMvc in the context of Spring Boot JUnit 5 tests?',options:['A real HTTP client for integration tests','A Spring MVC test framework that simulates HTTP requests through the DispatcherServlet without starting a real server','A Mockito-based web mock','A replacement for the embedded Tomcat'],answer:1,explanation:'MockMvc allows testing Spring MVC controllers without starting a full HTTP server. It simulates the full MVC request processing pipeline (dispatcher servlet, handler mapping, interceptors, view resolution). Combined with @WebMvcTest, it provides fast, focused controller testing.'},
{id:4952,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you configure MockMvc in a @SpringBootTest test?',options:['Inject MockMvc directly; it is auto-configured','Annotate the test class with @AutoConfigureMockMvc and inject MockMvc, or build it manually with MockMvcBuilders','Use @WebMvcTest only','Configure it in junit-platform.properties'],answer:1,explanation:'With @SpringBootTest, add @AutoConfigureMockMvc to auto-configure and inject MockMvc. Alternatively, use MockMvcBuilders.webAppContextSetup(webApplicationContext).build() manually. @WebMvcTest auto-configures MockMvc without @AutoConfigureMockMvc.'},
{id:4953,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between @WebMvcTest and @SpringBootTest with @AutoConfigureMockMvc?',options:['They are identical','@WebMvcTest loads only the MVC slice (fast); @SpringBootTest loads the full context (slower but more realistic)','@SpringBootTest does not support MockMvc','@WebMvcTest starts a real server'],answer:1,explanation:'@WebMvcTest loads only the MVC layer (controllers, filters, etc.) making it fast and focused. @SpringBootTest with @AutoConfigureMockMvc loads the entire application context, making it suitable for full integration tests where you need all beans wired together.'},
{id:4954,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What Maven/Gradle artifact provides MockitoExtension for use with JUnit 5?',options:['org.mockito:mockito-core','org.mockito:mockito-junit-jupiter','org.mockito:mockito-spring','org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter-mockito'],answer:1,explanation:'The MockitoExtension is provided by the mockito-junit-jupiter artifact (org.mockito:mockito-junit-jupiter). It depends on mockito-core and provides @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class) and related Mockito annotations for use with JUnit 5.'},
{id:4955,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Testcontainers do on a JUnit 5 test class?',options:['Imports Docker containers as Spring beans','Activates the Testcontainers JUnit 5 extension, which manages the lifecycle of @Container fields','Runs all tests inside Docker containers','Enables network isolation for tests'],answer:1,explanation:'@Testcontainers is a JUnit 5 extension annotation from the testcontainers-junit-jupiter module. It registers the TestcontainersExtension which discovers @Container fields and manages their start/stop lifecycle automatically during tests.'},
{id:4956,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the @Container annotation do in a Testcontainers JUnit 5 test?',options:['Defines the Docker image to use for the whole test suite','Marks a field as a Testcontainers container instance to be managed by the TestcontainersExtension','Creates a new Docker network for the test','Pins the container to a specific IP'],answer:1,explanation:'@Container marks a field of type GenericContainer (or a subclass like PostgreSQLContainer, MySQLContainer, etc.) for lifecycle management by the TestcontainersExtension. The container is started before the test and stopped after, or shared across tests if static.'},
{id:4957,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you share a Testcontainers container across all tests in a JUnit 5 class?',options:['Annotate the field with @Shared','Declare the @Container field as static','Use @BeforeAll to start the container','Use @TestInstance(PER_CLASS) only'],answer:1,explanation:'Declaring the @Container field as static causes the TestcontainersExtension to start the container before the first test in the class and stop it after the last. Instance (non-static) @Container fields result in a new container per test method.'},
{id:4958,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How can you share a Testcontainers container across multiple test classes in JUnit 5?',options:['Use @Container on a static field in each class','Use Testcontainers\'s Singleton Container pattern: create the container as a static field in a base class or a utility class and reuse it across test classes','Use @BeforeAll in a JUnit 5 extension','Set container.withReuse(true) and Testcontainers Desktop'],answer:1,explanation:'The Singleton Container pattern involves declaring the container in a static field in a base/abstract class or a utility class. The first test class that starts it keeps it alive for the JVM session. Testcontainers also supports container reuse with .withReuse(true) and a local Testcontainers configuration.'},
{id:4959,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which Testcontainers module provides a JUnit 5 @Container-aware PostgreSQL container?',options:['testcontainers:postgresql','org.testcontainers:postgresql','com.testcontainers:jdbc','testcontainers:jdbc'],answer:1,explanation:'The PostgreSQL Testcontainer is provided by the org.testcontainers:postgresql module. It provides PostgreSQLContainer which exposes getJdbcUrl(), getUsername(), and getPassword() methods for configuring the datasource in tests.'},
{id:4960,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of REST Assured in JUnit 5 tests?',options:['A JUnit 5 extension for testing REST controllers','A Java library that provides a DSL for testing and validating REST APIs by making HTTP requests and asserting responses','A Spring Boot test slice for REST endpoints','A Mockito extension for REST mocks'],answer:1,explanation:'REST Assured (io.rest-assured:rest-assured) provides a fluent Java DSL for HTTP testing: given().header(...).when().get("/api/users").then().statusCode(200).body("size()", greaterThan(0)). It can be used standalone or with Spring Boot\'s TestRestTemplate.'},
{id:4961,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you integrate REST Assured with Spring Boot and JUnit 5?',options:['Use @ExtendWith(RestAssuredExtension.class)','Configure RestAssured.baseURI and RestAssured.port from the @SpringBootTest-injected port, then use the DSL in @Test methods','Use @WebMvcTest with RestAssured','Import rest-assured-spring-mock-mvc for @WebMvcTest'],answer:1,explanation:'In a @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT) test, inject @LocalServerPort and set RestAssured.port = port; and RestAssured.baseURI = "http://localhost"; in @BeforeEach. Then use REST Assured\'s DSL in test methods for fluent end-to-end HTTP assertions.'},
{id:4962,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What Maven artifact provides REST Assured with Spring MockMvc support for slice tests?',options:['io.rest-assured:rest-assured','io.rest-assured:spring-mock-mvc','io.rest-assured:spring-web-test-client','io.rest-assured:spring-integration'],answer:1,explanation:'The io.rest-assured:spring-mock-mvc module integrates REST Assured with Spring\'s MockMvc. Use RestAssuredMockMvc.mockMvc(mockMvc) to configure it, then use the familiar given().when().get("/api").then() DSL without starting a real HTTP server.'},
{id:4963,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the default thread model for JUnit 5 test execution?',options:['All tests run in a thread pool by default','Tests run sequentially in a single thread by default','Each test class runs in its own thread','Tests run in threads based on the number of CPU cores'],answer:1,explanation:'By default, JUnit 5 executes tests sequentially in a single thread. Parallel execution must be explicitly enabled via configuration. This default ensures backward compatibility and test isolation without requiring thread-safety considerations in existing tests.'},
{id:4964,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you enable parallel test execution in JUnit 5?',options:['Add @Parallel to each test class','Set junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled=true in junit-platform.properties','Pass --parallel to the JVM','Use @TestMethodOrder(MethodOrderer.Parallel.class)'],answer:1,explanation:'Enable parallel execution by setting junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.enabled=true in junit-platform.properties. Additional configuration controls the mode: junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.default=concurrent (or same_thread) and the parallelism strategy.'},
{id:4965,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @Execution(ExecutionMode.CONCURRENT) do in JUnit 5?',options:['Makes only that test run concurrently with itself','Marks the test class or method to run concurrently with other tests when parallel execution is enabled','Forces sequential execution','Enables async test assertions'],answer:1,explanation:'@Execution(ExecutionMode.CONCURRENT) overrides the default execution mode for a test class or method. When parallel execution is enabled globally, annotating a class with this ensures its tests run concurrently. Use @Execution(SAME_THREAD) to opt out of parallelism for specific tests.'},
{id:4966,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the difference between DYNAMIC and FIXED parallel execution strategies in JUnit 5?',options:['DYNAMIC uses the fork/join pool; FIXED uses a custom thread pool','DYNAMIC adjusts the parallelism level based on available processors; FIXED uses a configured number of threads','They are the same','FIXED is for @RepeatedTest only'],answer:1,explanation:'JUnit 5 parallel execution offers several strategies via junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.config.strategy: FIXED (fixed number of threads configured by .fixed.parallelism), DYNAMIC (multiplier of available processors), and CUSTOM (user-defined ForkJoinPoolHierarchicalTestExecutorService factory).'},
{id:4967,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What annotation ensures a JUnit 5 test method runs on the same thread as the parent even when parallel execution is enabled?',options:['@SameThread','@Sequential','@Execution(ExecutionMode.SAME_THREAD)','@NotParallel'],answer:2,explanation:'@Execution(ExecutionMode.SAME_THREAD) forces the annotated test class or method to run on the same thread as its parent container. This is useful for tests that use thread-local state, non-thread-safe resources, or must interleave with other tests on the same thread.'},
{id:4968,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a potential issue when using assertTimeoutPreemptively() with Spring\'s @Transactional tests?',options:['Transactions are committed prematurely','The executable runs in a new thread that does not inherit the Spring-managed ThreadLocal transaction, potentially causing transactional behavior inconsistencies','Timeouts are ignored inside Spring contexts','Spring beans are re-created for each timeout check'],answer:1,explanation:'assertTimeoutPreemptively executes the code in a separate thread. Spring\'s @Transactional uses ThreadLocal to bind the current transaction. The new thread has no transaction context, so transactional operations inside the executable may fail or operate outside the test\'s transaction boundary.'},
{id:4969,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the ExtensionContext.Store API provide for JUnit 5 extensions?',options:['A type-safe map for storing and sharing data within and across extension lifecycles','A way to store mock objects','A persistence layer for test results','Thread-local storage for parallel tests'],answer:0,explanation:'ExtensionContext.Store is a namespace-scoped key-value store. Extensions use getStore(Namespace.create(MyExtension.class)) to obtain a Store and put/get/remove values. Stores are tied to the extension context\'s lifecycle, auto-closing CloseableResource values when the context closes.'},
{id:4970,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What does implementing CloseableResource on a Store-managed object do in JUnit 5?',options:['It marks the object as immutable','JUnit automatically calls close() on the object when the associated ExtensionContext closes, enabling automatic resource cleanup','It prevents the object from being garbage collected','It serializes the object to disk'],answer:1,explanation:'If an object stored in an ExtensionContext.Store implements Store.CloseableResource, JUnit calls its close() method when the extension context closes (e.g., at end of test method or test class). This enables automatic resource cleanup without explicit teardown code in the extension.'},
{id:4971,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Can @ExtendWith be applied multiple times to the same test class in JUnit 5?',options:['No, only one extension per class','Yes, @ExtendWith is repeatable and multiple extensions can be registered on the same class','Yes, but only two at a time','Only if extensions implement Combinable'],answer:1,explanation:'@ExtendWith is a repeatable annotation in JUnit 5. You can stack multiple @ExtendWith declarations: @ExtendWith(ExtA.class) @ExtendWith(ExtB.class). Extensions are invoked in order from outermost to innermost for setup and innermost to outermost for teardown.'},
{id:4972,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a @SpringBootTest with @MockBean, when is the mock reset?',options:['After the entire test suite','After each test method, before the next test method runs','Only when explicitly reset with Mockito.reset()','After each assertion'],answer:1,explanation:'@MockBean mocks are automatically reset after each test method (before the next one starts). Spring Boot\'s MockitoResetTestExecutionListener handles this reset, ensuring that stubbings, verifications, and invocation counts from one test do not leak into another.'},
{id:4973,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @LocalServerPort do in a @SpringBootTest test?',options:['Sets the server port to a fixed value','Injects the actual port that the embedded server is listening on, useful when webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT','Mocks the server port','Sets the local port for outbound connections'],answer:1,explanation:'@LocalServerPort (or @Value("${local.server.port}")) injects the randomly assigned port when using @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment=RANDOM_PORT). This allows you to configure REST clients, TestRestTemplate, or REST Assured to send requests to the correct port.'},
{id:4974,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DataJpaTest do to the database by default?',options:['Uses the production database configuration','Replaces the configured DataSource with an embedded in-memory database','Uses a remote test database','Disables the DataSource entirely'],answer:1,explanation:'@DataJpaTest replaces the application DataSource with an embedded database (H2, HSQLDB, or Derby) by default. This gives fast, isolated database tests. Use @AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace=NONE) to disable this replacement and use the real database configuration.'},
{id:4975,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does @Testcontainers work with Spring Boot\'s @DynamicPropertySource to configure datasource properties?',options:['Testcontainers sets Spring properties automatically','@DynamicPropertySource adds a property source that uses container-provided connection details (e.g., getJdbcUrl()) at context startup, allowing Spring Boot to connect to the containerized database','@SpringBootTest reads container properties from environment variables','Testcontainers creates a DataSource bean directly'],answer:1,explanation:'@DynamicPropertySource on a static method in the test class allows registering dynamic properties before the context starts. container.getJdbcUrl(), getUsername(), and getPassword() are used to set spring.datasource.url and related properties, enabling Spring Boot to connect to the Testcontainers-managed database.'},
{id:4976,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of @ConditionalOnJre in the context of JUnit 5 conditional annotations?',options:['It is a Spring Boot autoconfiguration annotation unrelated to JUnit','@DisabledOnJre and @EnabledOnJre serve the JUnit conditional purpose; @ConditionalOnJre is not a JUnit annotation','It enables JRE detection for all tests globally','It is a JUnit 5 alias for @EnabledOnJre'],answer:1,explanation:'@ConditionalOnJre is a Spring Boot autoconfiguration annotation, not a JUnit 5 annotation. JUnit 5 provides @EnabledOnJre and @DisabledOnJre for JRE-based conditional test execution. Confusing Spring Boot and JUnit annotations is a common mistake to avoid.'},
{id:4977,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @EnabledIfSystemProperty(named="myFlag", matches="true") do if the system property is not set?',options:['The test runs normally','The test is disabled (skipped)','The test fails','The test throws an exception'],answer:1,explanation:'If the system property "myFlag" is not set, @EnabledIfSystemProperty treats the condition as not met and disables the test (it is skipped/aborted). The property must both exist and its value must match the given regex for the test to be enabled.'},
{id:4978,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which JUnit 5 annotation would you use to run a test only when an environment variable named "INTEGRATION_TESTS" equals "true"?',options:['@EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable(named="INTEGRATION_TESTS", matches="true")','@RunIfEnv("INTEGRATION_TESTS=true")','@Conditional(envVar="INTEGRATION_TESTS", value="true")','@EnabledIfSystemProperty(named="INTEGRATION_TESTS", matches="true")'],answer:0,explanation:'@EnabledIfEnvironmentVariable(named="INTEGRATION_TESTS", matches="true") enables the test only when the environment variable INTEGRATION_TESTS is set and its value matches the regex "true". Environment variables are typically set in CI/CD pipelines to control test execution.'},
{id:4979,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How do you compose multiple conditional annotations in JUnit 5?',options:['Use @ComposedCondition combining them','JUnit evaluates multiple condition annotations with AND logic; all conditions must be met for the test to run','JUnit evaluates them with OR logic','Only one conditional annotation is allowed per test'],answer:1,explanation:'When multiple condition annotations are applied to a test, JUnit 5 evaluates them with AND semantics: all conditions must be satisfied for the test to run. If any condition is not met, the test is disabled. This allows building complex conditions from simpler building blocks.'},
{id:4980,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the MockMvc perform() method accept?',options:['A String URL','A RequestBuilder (e.g., MockMvcRequestBuilders.get("/api/users"))','A RestTemplate','A ResponseEntity'],answer:1,explanation:'MockMvc.perform(RequestBuilder requestBuilder) accepts a RequestBuilder such as MockMvcRequestBuilders.get("/url"), post("/url"), put("/url"), etc. It returns a ResultActions on which you can chain andExpect() calls to verify the response status, headers, and body.'},
{id:4981,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What assertion method on MockMvc ResultActions checks the HTTP response status code?',options:['andExpect(status().isOk())','andVerify(status(200))','andAssert(HttpStatus.OK)','andCheck(statusCode(200))'],answer:0,explanation:'mockMvc.perform(get("/api")).andExpect(status().isOk()) uses MockMvcResultMatchers.status() to assert the HTTP status code is 200. Other matchers include status().isCreated(), status().isBadRequest(), and status().is(int expectedStatus).'},
{id:4982,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In a @WebMvcTest, why do you typically need @MockBean for service layers?',options:['@WebMvcTest includes services by default','@WebMvcTest only loads MVC components; service beans are not loaded, so @MockBean provides mock implementations that controllers can depend on','Services are auto-proxied in @WebMvcTest','@MockBean is optional in @WebMvcTest'],answer:1,explanation:'@WebMvcTest deliberately excludes @Service and @Repository beans to keep the test focused and fast. Any service that a controller depends on must be provided via @MockBean (a Mockito mock in the Spring context), otherwise the test context will fail to start due to unsatisfied dependencies.'},
{id:4983,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is a Testcontainers JdbcDatabaseContainer and what advantage does it provide?',options:['A JDBC connection pool for tests','A specialized Testcontainers container that exposes getJdbcUrl(), getUsername(), and getPassword(), enabling easy datasource configuration for database integration tests','A mock JDBC driver','An in-memory JDBC database'],answer:1,explanation:'JdbcDatabaseContainer is a base class for database containers like PostgreSQLContainer, MySQLContainer, etc. It provides getJdbcUrl(), getUsername(), getPassword(), and getDatabaseName() which align with Spring Boot DataSource configuration properties, simplifying test datasource setup.'},
{id:4984,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @AutoConfigureMockMvc do in a @SpringBootTest test?',options:['Creates a MockMvc instance configured against the full Spring application context and injects it','Only works with @WebMvcTest','Disables MockMvc for the test','Creates a standalone MockMvc without the full context'],answer:0,explanation:'@AutoConfigureMockMvc, used alongside @SpringBootTest, auto-configures MockMvc to dispatch through the full Spring application context (including security, interceptors, and all beans). This gives a more realistic test than @WebMvcTest while still not requiring a real HTTP server.'},
{id:4985,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Can a JUnit 5 extension implement multiple callback interfaces at once?',options:['No, each extension can only implement one interface','Yes, an extension class can implement multiple callback interfaces like BeforeEachCallback, AfterEachCallback, and ParameterResolver simultaneously','Only two interfaces per extension','Only if the interfaces have no method conflicts'],answer:1,explanation:'A single JUnit 5 extension class can implement multiple callback interfaces. For example, a database extension might implement BeforeAllCallback (to start the DB), BeforeEachCallback (to clean data), AfterEachCallback (for verification), and ParameterResolver (to inject a connection). This consolidates related cross-cutting concerns.'},
{id:4986,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the ExtensionContext hierarchy in JUnit 5?',options:['A flat list of extension contexts','A tree structure where each context represents a node (suite, class, method) with access to parent contexts, enabling extensions to store and retrieve data at the appropriate scope','A single global context for all tests','An immutable snapshot taken once before any tests'],answer:1,explanation:'ExtensionContext forms a hierarchy: the engine context at the root, then class contexts, then method contexts. Extensions can navigate to parent contexts to access or store class-level data. The getParent() method traverses up the tree, while getRoot() reaches the engine-level context.'},
{id:4987,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In parallel JUnit 5 tests, what is the risk of using static mutable state in test classes?',options:['Static state is automatically isolated by JUnit','Concurrent test methods from different classes may read/write the same static field simultaneously, causing race conditions and non-deterministic failures','Static fields are thread-local by default','Parallel execution locks static fields'],answer:1,explanation:'When tests run in parallel (multiple threads), static mutable fields shared across test classes or methods are accessed concurrently without synchronization. This causes race conditions, flaky tests, and subtle failures that are hard to reproduce. Prefer instance fields and proper test isolation.'},
{id:4988,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @ResourceLock do in JUnit 5 parallel execution?',options:['Locks a JVM resource to prevent GC','Declares a named shared resource that tests need exclusive or read access to, enabling safe parallel execution without race conditions','Locks the test class from parallel modification','Prevents a test from running in parallel with itself'],answer:1,explanation:'@ResourceLock("myResource") declares that the annotated test needs access to a named shared resource. Tests with READ mode can run in parallel with each other, but READ_WRITE (default) gives exclusive access. This prevents race conditions in parallel tests that share state.'},
{id:4989,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'Which JUnit 5 API would you use to build a custom test engine for a custom testing language?',options:['TestSuite API','TestEngine SPI (org.junit.platform.engine.TestEngine)','Extension API','TestWatcher'],answer:1,explanation:'To support a custom testing language or framework on the JUnit Platform, implement the org.junit.platform.engine.TestEngine interface, which requires discover(EngineDiscoveryRequest, UniqueId) and execute(ExecutionRequest) methods. Register it via ServiceLoader in META-INF/services/org.junit.platform.engine.TestEngine.'},
{id:4990,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DisabledIf in JUnit 5 (5.7+) do?',options:['Permanently disables the test','Disables the test based on the return value of a specified method that returns boolean','Disables the test if a specific dependency is missing','Disables all tests in a package'],answer:1,explanation:'@DisabledIf("methodName") is the inverse of @EnabledIf. The test is disabled when the referenced method returns true. The method must return boolean and be accessible (package-private or higher) in the test class or a referenced external class.'},
{id:4991,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'How do you configure the parallelism level in JUnit 5 when using the FIXED strategy?',options:['junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.config.fixed.parallelism=4','junit.jupiter.threads=4','@Parallel(threads=4)','junit.platform.parallel.fixed=4'],answer:0,explanation:'With strategy=fixed, set junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.config.fixed.parallelism=N in junit-platform.properties (where N is the desired thread count). The DYNAMIC strategy uses junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.config.dynamic.factor (default 1.0) multiplied by available processors.'},
{id:4992,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the TestWatcher extension interface in JUnit 5 used for?',options:['Watches for file system changes during tests','Receives callbacks for test events: testSuccessful, testFailed, testAborted, testDisabled, enabling observation without modifying test behavior','Replaces the JUnit test runner','Watches for memory leaks during test execution'],answer:1,explanation:'TestWatcher (since JUnit 5.4) provides callbacks testSuccessful(ExtensionContext), testFailed(ExtensionContext, Throwable), testAborted(ExtensionContext, Throwable), and testDisabled(ExtensionContext, Optional<String>). Extensions implement it to react to test outcomes for logging, reporting, or retry logic.'},
{id:4993,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which org.junit.jupiter.api.condition annotation would you use to disable a test on Java 8 but enable it on Java 11+?',options:['@EnabledOnJre({JRE.JAVA_11, JRE.JAVA_17, JRE.JAVA_21})','@DisabledOnJre(JRE.JAVA_8)','@EnabledForJavaVersions(min=11)','Both A and B are correct'],answer:3,explanation:'Both @EnabledOnJre({JRE.JAVA_11, JRE.JAVA_17, JRE.JAVA_21}) and @DisabledOnJre(JRE.JAVA_8) would effectively disable the test on Java 8. @DisabledOnJre is more succinct for this case, while @EnabledOnJre requires explicitly listing all supported versions.'},
{id:4994,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT) configure?',options:['Starts the embedded server on a random port above 1024, with the port injectable via @LocalServerPort','Disables web configuration','Runs tests without a server but assigns a random port internally','Uses a random port from a properties file'],answer:0,explanation:'RANDOM_PORT starts the actual embedded web server on an available random port. This enables true HTTP-level integration testing. The port is exposed via @LocalServerPort injection. Use NONE for no web server (default), MOCK for a mock servlet environment, or DEFINED_PORT for the configured port.'},
{id:4995,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What happens to the Spring ApplicationContext when @MockBean is used in a @SpringBootTest?',options:['The context is not affected','Adding @MockBean causes a new ApplicationContext to be created (the modified context is not cached), which can slow down test suites with many different @MockBean configurations','The context is reloaded before each test','@MockBean disables Spring context caching entirely'],answer:1,explanation:'Spring\'s test context caching works by reusing contexts with the same configuration. Adding @MockBean modifies the context configuration, creating a context unique to that test class. If multiple test classes use different @MockBean combinations, multiple contexts are created, increasing test suite startup time.'},
{id:4996,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of @Import in a @WebMvcTest or @DataJpaTest test?',options:['Imports CSV data into the database','Adds additional Spring configuration classes or beans to the limited slice context that are not auto-detected','Imports test fixtures from another module','Imports Mockito configuration'],answer:1,explanation:'Slice tests load a limited subset of beans. @Import(MyConfigClass.class) adds additional configuration, beans, or components that the slice does not auto-detect. For example, @WebMvcTest might need @Import(SecurityConfig.class) to test secured endpoints, or custom argument resolvers.'},
{id:4997,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the @ContainerMatches annotation in Testcontainers provide?',options:['Verifies the container image version','This is not a real Testcontainers annotation; container assertions are done via JDBC/API clients against the running container','Asserts that a container is running','Matches a container to a Spring profile'],answer:1,explanation:'@ContainerMatches is not a real Testcontainers annotation. Assertions against Testcontainers are made by interacting with the container\'s exposed services (JDBC connections, HTTP endpoints, etc.) using regular JUnit 5 assertions. Testcontainers itself provides lifecycle management, not assertion utilities.'},
{id:4998,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'How does Testcontainers ensure containers are stopped even if a test fails or throws an exception?',options:['It uses a JVM shutdown hook','The TestcontainersExtension implements AfterEachCallback and AfterAllCallback to stop containers, and Ryuk (a sidecar container) cleans up any orphaned containers if the JVM crashes','Containers run until the OS restarts','Testcontainers requires manual container.stop() calls'],answer:1,explanation:'TestcontainersExtension manages container lifecycle via JUnit extension callbacks. Additionally, Testcontainers starts the Ryuk container (a sidecar) that monitors the test JVM. If the JVM exits (even abnormally), Ryuk removes any containers that were started by that session, preventing resource leaks.'},
{id:4999,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'In JUnit 5, what does the ConditionEvaluationResult.enabled("reason") return value indicate in a custom ExecutionCondition?',options:['The test should be skipped','The test is disabled for the given reason','The test should run; it is enabled','The test evaluation encountered an error'],answer:2,explanation:'ExecutionCondition.evaluateExecutionCondition(ExtensionContext) returns ConditionEvaluationResult. Returning ConditionEvaluationResult.enabled("reason") means the condition is satisfied and the test will run. Returning .disabled("reason") skips the test with the provided reason.'},
{id:5000,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What interface do you implement to create a custom conditional execution annotation in JUnit 5?',options:['ConditionalExtension','ExecutionCondition','TestCondition','ConditionalAnnotation'],answer:1,explanation:'To create a custom condition like @EnabledOnMyEnv, implement org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExecutionCondition which defines evaluateExecutionCondition(ExtensionContext context) returning ConditionEvaluationResult. Register it via @ExtendWith(MyConditionExtension.class) on your custom annotation.'},
{id:5001,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DisabledOnOs({OS.LINUX, OS.MAC}) do in JUnit 5?',options:['Disables the test only on Windows','Disables the test when running on either Linux or macOS, enabling it on all other operating systems','Disables all tests in the class on Linux and Mac','Throws an exception on non-specified OS'],answer:1,explanation:'@DisabledOnOs accepts one or more OS enum values. The test is disabled when the current operating system matches any of the specified values. Using {OS.LINUX, OS.MAC} disables it on both platforms while allowing it to run on Windows, AIX, Solaris, etc.'},
{id:5002,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the purpose of the JUnit 5 @Tag("slow") annotation in a CI/CD pipeline?',options:['Marks tests as expected to take more than 1 second','Provides a label that CI tools can use to exclude slow tests from the fast feedback cycle and run them in a separate slower build stage','Automatically parallelizes tagged tests','Sets a timeout for tagged tests'],answer:1,explanation:'@Tag("slow") labels tests that take a long time. CI/CD pipelines can be configured to exclude @Tag("slow") tests from the fast unit test stage (using Maven Surefire\'s excludedGroups) and include them only in a slower integration stage, balancing feedback speed and coverage.'},
{id:5003,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'Which JUnit 5 extension interface allows intercepting constructor invocations of test classes?',options:['ConstructorInterceptor','InvocationInterceptor (interceptTestClassConstructor method)','BeforeAllCallback','TestInstanceFactory'],answer:1,explanation:'InvocationInterceptor.interceptTestClassConstructor(Invocation<T>, ReflectiveInvocationContext<Constructor<T>>, ExtensionContext) intercepts the instantiation of the test class. Calling invocation.proceed() executes the real constructor. This is rarely needed but useful for constructor injection scenarios.'},
{id:5004,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is TestInstanceFactory in JUnit 5?',options:['A factory for creating @TestFactory tests','An extension interface allowing full control over how test class instances are created, enabling custom dependency injection frameworks to provide test instances','A class that creates DynamicTest instances','A factory for creating mock test instances'],answer:1,explanation:'TestInstanceFactory (since JUnit 5.3) allows an extension to take full control of test class instantiation. Its createTestInstance(TestInstanceFactoryContext, ExtensionContext) method must return the test instance. Only one TestInstanceFactory is allowed per test class hierarchy.'},
{id:5005,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @DisabledInNativeImage do in JUnit 5 (5.9+)?',options:['Disables tests when running with a JIT compiler','Disables tests when running as a GraalVM native image, where certain reflection-based features may not be available','Disables tests on native ARM processors','Disables tests that use native methods'],answer:1,explanation:'@DisabledInNativeImage (JUnit 5.9+) is a conditional annotation that disables a test when it is running inside a GraalVM native image executable. This is useful for tests that rely on Java reflection, proxies, or other features that may not be fully supported in native image environments.'},
{id:5006,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When using @Testcontainers with a static @Container field, at what point does the container start?',options:['When the test class is compiled','Before the first test method in the class executes (in BeforeAllCallback), and stops after the last test method','Before each test method','When the JVM starts'],answer:1,explanation:'A static @Container field is managed at the class level. The TestcontainersExtension starts the container once (via BeforeAllCallback semantics) before any test in the class runs, and stops it once (via AfterAllCallback semantics) after all tests complete, minimizing container startup overhead.'},
{id:5007,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the Spring Boot TestEntityManager and when would you use it in a @DataJpaTest?',options:['A mock for EntityManager','A JPA EntityManager wrapper provided by Spring Boot that is optimized for tests, offering helper methods like persistFlushFind() for common test scenarios','A Spring Data repository alternative','A JPA auditing configuration for tests'],answer:1,explanation:'TestEntityManager is provided in @DataJpaTest contexts. It wraps JPA\'s EntityManager and adds test-friendly helpers: persistFlushFind(entity) persists, flushes, clears the context, and reloads—simulating a full round-trip to test that entities are correctly persisted and reloaded from the database.'},
{id:5008,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What is the effect of @Transactional on a @DataJpaTest test method?',options:['No effect; @DataJpaTest manages transactions separately','The test runs in a transaction that is rolled back after the method completes, leaving the database in its original state','The transaction is committed after each assertion','Transactions are disabled in @DataJpaTest'],answer:1,explanation:'@DataJpaTest applies @Transactional by default on all test methods. After each test method, the transaction is rolled back, undoing any database changes. This ensures tests are independent and do not affect each other\'s data, avoiding the need for manual cleanup.'},
{id:5009,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What artifact must be added to use Testcontainers JUnit 5 support?',options:['org.testcontainers:testcontainers','org.testcontainers:junit-jupiter','com.testcontainers:core','org.junit.testcontainers:extension'],answer:1,explanation:'The JUnit 5 Testcontainers integration is provided by org.testcontainers:junit-jupiter. This artifact provides the @Testcontainers class annotation and @Container field annotation. It depends on org.testcontainers:testcontainers for the container management core.'},
{id:5010,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does REST Assured\'s given().auth().basic("user","pass") configure?',options:['Creates a mock user for Spring Security tests','Configures HTTP Basic authentication credentials for the REST Assured request','Creates a JWT token','Configures SSL certificate'],answer:1,explanation:'given().auth().basic("username","password") adds an HTTP Basic Authorization header to the REST Assured request. This is used in integration tests against secured endpoints to authenticate. REST Assured also supports OAuth2, form authentication, and digest authentication.'},
{id:5011,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does MockMvc\'s andDo(print()) do in a JUnit 5 Spring Boot test?',options:['Prints the test result to a report file','Prints the full request and response details (headers, body, status) to System.out for debugging','Generates an HTML test report','Prints only the response body'],answer:1,explanation:'andDo(MockMvcResultHandlers.print()) logs the complete MockMvc request and response information to System.out, including method, URL, parameters, headers, body, and status code. It is extremely useful for debugging test failures without starting a real server.'},
{id:5012,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'In JUnit 5 parallel execution, what does junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.classes.default=concurrent configure?',options:['Makes test methods within a class run concurrently','Makes test classes themselves run concurrently with each other while test methods within a class run sequentially by default','Makes all tests in all classes concurrent','Applies only to @Nested classes'],answer:1,explanation:'junit.jupiter.execution.parallel.mode.classes.default=concurrent controls the execution mode for top-level test classes. Setting it to concurrent runs classes in parallel. The complementary property mode.default controls whether test methods within a class run concurrently or sequentially.'},
{id:5013,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @SpringBootTest with classes attribute do?',options:['Specifies the test class to load','Specifies which @Configuration classes or @SpringBootApplication class to load instead of auto-detecting','Limits tests to specified test classes','Imports JUnit test suites'],answer:1,explanation:'@SpringBootTest(classes = {AppConfig.class, SecurityConfig.class}) explicitly specifies the configuration classes to load. Without this, Spring Boot searches for @SpringBootApplication in the package hierarchy. Specifying classes gives explicit control for modular tests.'},
{id:5014,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the REST Assured then().body("$.name", equalTo("Alice")) assertion do?',options:['Checks that the response body is exactly the string "$.name"','Uses JsonPath expression "$.name" to extract the value from the JSON response body and asserts it equals "Alice"','Checks that a JSON field named "name" is present','Verifies the request body'],answer:1,explanation:'then().body(jsonPath, matcher) extracts a value from the JSON response using a GPath/JsonPath expression and applies a Hamcrest matcher. "$.name" (or just "name" in Groovy notation) extracts the top-level "name" field. This enables concise, readable JSON response assertions.'},
{id:5015,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the purpose of @TestPropertySource in a Spring Boot JUnit 5 test?',options:['Sources test data from a file','Adds or overrides Spring properties for the test, loading from a file or inline key-value pairs, allowing environment-specific configuration in tests','Configures the test reporter','Defines test execution properties for JUnit Platform'],answer:1,explanation:'@TestPropertySource(properties = {"spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:test"}) or @TestPropertySource(locations = "classpath:test.properties") adds properties to the Spring environment for the test context. These override default application.properties and are useful for test-specific configuration.'},
{id:5016,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does @ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class) do and is it necessary with @SpringBootTest?',options:['It is always required separately','SpringExtension integrates Spring TestContext Framework with JUnit 5; @SpringBootTest already includes it via @BootstrapWith, making it redundant but harmless when added explicitly','It is required for @DataJpaTest only','It replaces @ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)'],answer:1,explanation:'SpringExtension is the JUnit 5 integration for Spring\'s TestContext Framework. @SpringBootTest, @WebMvcTest, @DataJpaTest, and other Spring slice annotations are meta-annotated with @ExtendWith(SpringExtension.class), so you don\'t need to add it manually. Adding it is redundant but not harmful.'},
{id:5017,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'What does the Testcontainers withReuse(true) method enable?',options:['Shares containers between parallel test threads','Enables container reuse between test runs, preventing container recreation on each test execution if the container configuration has not changed','Automatically restarts failed containers','Enables container health checks'],answer:1,explanation:'container.withReuse(true) marks a container to be reused across test runs. Testcontainers identifies containers by their hash and reuses an already-running container if it matches. This significantly speeds up repeated test runs. Requires testcontainers.reuse.enable=true in ~/.testcontainers.properties.'},
{id:5018,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the primary difference between a JUnit 5 extension\'s BeforeAllCallback and a test class\'s @BeforeAll method?',options:['They are identical in behavior','BeforeAllCallback runs before any @BeforeAll methods in the test class, and the extension runs its logic without access to the test instance (which may not exist with PER_METHOD lifecycle)','@BeforeAll runs before BeforeAllCallback','They run concurrently'],answer:1,explanation:'BeforeAllCallback in an extension executes before the @BeforeAll method in the test class. The extension\'s callback provides the ExtensionContext but not a direct test instance (with PER_METHOD lifecycle, no single instance exists). This distinction matters when the extension needs to set up class-level state before @BeforeAll logic runs.'},
{id:5019,topic:'junit',difficulty:'intermediate',question:'When should you prefer @SpyBean over @MockBean in a Spring Boot test?',options:['When you need full mock control','When you want the real implementation but with the ability to verify interactions or stub specific methods, keeping most behavior real','When the bean is in a @Configuration class','When the bean has no dependencies'],answer:1,explanation:'Use @SpyBean when you need the real bean behavior for most methods (important for integration-level testing) but want to stub specific methods or verify that certain methods were called. Use @MockBean when you want complete control via stubbing and need to isolate the component under test from its dependencies.'},
{id:5020,topic:'junit',difficulty:'advanced',question:'What is the role of the JUnit Platform Launcher API and how is it used outside of build tools?',options:['It is only for Maven and Gradle','The Launcher API allows programmatic discovery and execution of tests from application code, CI tools, or custom test frameworks via LauncherFactory.create() and LauncherDiscoveryRequestBuilder','It provides the command line interface only','It is used for configuring test extensions'],answer:1,explanation:'The Launcher API (org.junit.platform.launcher) allows any Java application to discover and run JUnit tests programmatically. Create a request with LauncherDiscoveryRequestBuilder selecting tests by class, package, or tag, then execute it with Launcher.execute(request, listeners). This powers IDE integrations and custom test runners.'},
]);
